------NOTE: COMMANDS IN DOCKERFILE
WORKDIR - BASIC SYNTAX: WORKDIR /path/to/directory
The WORKDIR instruction is used in Dockerfiles to set the working directory for any subsequent instructions (like RUN, CMD, ENTRYPOINT, COPY, and ADD).
When Docker encounters a WORKDIR instruction, it does three things:
    - Creates the directory if it doesn't already exist inside the container's filesystem.
    - Changes the current directory to that path for all commands that come after it in the Dockerfile.
    - Persists as the default directory when you run the container interactively (e.g., docker run -it my-image will drop you into this directory).
# When you use cd instead of WORKDIR, that cd command runs in a temporary shell session. As soon as that shell session ends
 (which happens right after the RUN command finishes), the directory resets back to where it was.
NB: /usr/src/app (descriptive) or /app (modern and simple)

RUN :  used to Execute commands to build your image layers (e.g., installing packages, compiling code, changing permissions).
it is used at build-time (when you run docker build .) # can't be overridden, its baked in.
 Syntax: Two forms.
   - Shell form: RUN apt-get install -y python3 (runs in /bin/sh -c)
   - Exec form: RUN ["apt-get", "install", "-y", "python3"] (preferred for security, no shell variable expansion).

CMD (Runtime - Default) : Purpose: Provides defaults for an executing container. It tells Docker "If the user doesn't specify a command, run this."
When it runs: At container startup (docker run).
Can you override it at runtime? Yes, easily. If you run docker run my-image bash, the bash completely replaces the CMD.
 Syntax:
   - Exec form (PREFERRED): CMD ["npm", "start"]
   - Shell form: CMD npm start (starts a sub-shell, less ideal for signal handling).
As ENTRYPOINT parameters: CMD ["--port", "8080"] (used to pass default arguments to ENTRYPOINT).Important: If your Dockerfile has multiple CMD instructions, only the last one takes effect.

ENTRYPOINT (Runtime - Immutable)
Purpose: Configures the container to run as an executable. It defines the main program that should always run.
When it runs: At container startup.
Can you override it at runtime? Yes, but you must use a flag: docker run --entrypoint bash my-image.
Syntax:
  - Exec form (PREFERRED): ENTRYPOINT ["python", "app.py"]
  - Shell form: ENTRYPOINT python app.py (rarely used).
 ENTRYPOINT + CMD combination :
ENTRYPOINT ["curl"]      # The program is ALWAYS curl #defines the static program (the binary).
CMD ["--help"]           # If user types nothing, run "curl --help" #defines the dynamic default arguments passed to that program.

RUN vs CMD vs ENTRYPOINT
Instruction     	Time	     Can User Override?  	Use Case
RUN	               Build	         No	              Installing dependencies (apt-get, pip install)
CMD	               Start	       Yes (easily)	      Setting default commands or arguments
ENTRYPOINT	       Start	     Only with --entrypoint	Defining the main application binary

COPY (Simple & Safe) Purpose: The standard, safest way to copy local files into an image.
What it does: Copies files/directories from your build context (the directory where you run docker build) to the container.
Best Practice: Use COPY 95% of the time. It is transparent and predictable.
Syntax: COPY <source> <destination>
   * COPY package.json /app/ (Copies a single file)
   * COPY . /app (Copies everything from your current directory into /app)

ADD (Powerful but Risky) - Purpose: Does everything COPY does, PLUS extra automatic features.
Extra Features:
Auto-extract local tarballs: If you ADD myarchive.tar.gz /app/, Docker will automatically unzip/un-tar it inside the container.
Remote URLs: You can download files from the internet directly: ADD https://example.com/file.zip /tmp/ 
    # (Note: This is strongly discouraged because it adds an extra layer, slows down builds, and is insecure).
When to use it: Only when you need to automatically extract a local compressed archive into the image.
*ADD is discouraged:
 - Lack of transparency: You might forget that a .tar.gz file gets auto-extracted, leading to unexpected folder structures.
 - Invalidation of cache: Downloading from URLs breaks Docker's layer caching (because the remote file might change).
 - Security: Downloading from the internet during a build is risky. Use RUN curl ... instead so you can clean up after yourself in the same layer.
