This image file is built from an instructional file named Dockerfile that is parsed when you run docker image build run:
 (syntax) * docker buildx build [OPTIONS] PATH | URL |
docker build flags go into these categories:
General build:
-f, --file: Tells Docker to use a specific Dockerfile instead of the default Dockerfile. 
Useful when you have multiple Dockerfiles (e.g., Dockerfile.dev, Dockerfile.prod) or keep them in subdirectories.
* docker build -f Dockerfile.dev .	

 --build-arg: Passes build-time variables into the Dockerfile (used with ARG instructions). 
This is for non-sensitive values (e.g., version numbers). Do not use for secrets—they remain in the image history.
* docker build --build-arg VERSION=1.2.3 .
NB.  --build-arg for passwords or API keys. They are stored in the image history (docker history --no-trunc). 
Use --secret (BuildKit) or secret mounts instead.
#BuildKit is the modern, high-performance backend engine for docker build that has replaced the older legacy builder
 Docker Build uses a client-server model: 
   - Client (Buildx): The command-line interface you interact with when you run docker build. 
   It interprets your command and sends a build request to the backend .
   - Server (BuildKit): The actual "builder" daemon that receives the request, executes the build steps, and produces the final image . 
    You can even have multiple BuildKit backends (called "builders") managed by a single client.
   - You get: better cache invalidation, Parallel layer building, --secret and --ssh support, Skipping unused stages.
 # BuildKit is enabled by default in modern Docker installations . However, if you need to explicitly enable or disable it
    (e.g., to use the legacy builder for Windows containers), you can use the DOCKER_BUILDKIT environment variable :
    * export DOCKER_BUILDKIT=1 docker build -t myapp . # Enable BuildKit
    * export DOCKER_BUILDKIT=0 docker build -t myapp . # Disable BuildKit (use legacy builder)

--secret:	(BuildKit only) Securely passes sensitive data (API keys, passwords) into the build without persisting them in the final image. 
Much safer than --build-arg for secrets. * docker build --secret id=mysecret,src=./key.txt .

--target: Builds up to a specific stage in a multi-stage Dockerfile. Stops at that stage instead of building the final image. 
Great for debugging intermediate layers or building only a dev environment. * docker build --target builder .

Cache & Optimization Flags:
--no-cache:	Forces Docker to ignore the entire build cache. Every instruction runs fresh. 
Useful when you suspect a corrupted cache or need a completely clean rebuild (but slows down builds significantly).
* docker build --no-cache .

--pull: Forces Docker to always pull the latest version of the base image (FROM) before building. 
Without this, Docker uses whatever base image you have locally, which might be outdated.
* docker build --pull .	

--cache-from: (BuildKit) Tells Docker to use a specific image as a cache source—even if it wasn't built locally. 
Essential for CI/CD pipelines where you want to reuse layers from a previously pushed image to speed up builds.
* docker build --cache-from myapp:latest .

--memory:	Limits the maximum memory the build can use. Prevents a single build from consuming all host RAM.
* docker build --memory=2g .

Output & Metadata Flags
-t, --tag: The -t flag stands for tag and is used to give a Docker image a human-readable name and optional version (tag) when you build it. Th
is makes it easy to identify and run the image later, for example myapp:latest or myapp:v2. *	docker build -t myapp:1.0 -t myapp:latest .	

--label: Adds metadata to the image in key=value format. Useful for adding version, build date, or maintainer info. Can be inspected later with docker inspect.
*	docker build --label maintainer="john@example.com" .	

--output, -o	docker: (BuildKit) Exports build artifacts (files, binaries) directly to the host filesystem without creating a Docker image. 
Fantastic for compiling code inside a container and extracting the result. * build --output type=local,dest=./dist .	

Advanced / BuildKit-Only Flags (Modern Docker)
--ssh:	(BuildKit) Forwards your SSH agent into the build container. 
Allows RUN commands to securely clone private Git repos during build without embedding SSH keys in the image.
* docker build --ssh default .

--platform:	Builds an image for a different architecture than your host (e.g., building ARM64 on an x86 machine).
Requires emulation (QEMU) or a builder that supports cross-platform builds. *	docker build --platform linux/arm64 .

--progress:	Controls the output verbosity. auto (default) shows a fancy UI; plain shows raw, line-by-line logs (great for debugging); 
tty forces interactive. *docker build --progress=plain .

--no-cache-filter: (BuildKit) Clears the cache only for specific stages in a multi-stage build, while preserving cache for others.
Saves time compared to --no-cache. * docker build --no-cache-filter stage1 .	




